{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a5422074eb45b748", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t                                                            T EFFIC -AUDIO : T ELL FACT FROM F ICTION\n2\t\n3\t                                                                                         T ECHNICAL R EPORT\n4\t\n5\t\n6\t                                                                                            Amphion Team\n7\t\n8\t\n9\t\n10\t\n11\t                                                                                             A BSTRACT\n12\t                                                  Speech deepfake detection has expanded in scope with increasingly heterogeneous spoofing mech-\n13\t                                                  anisms, including speech synthesis, voice conversion, vocoder reconstruction, and neural-codec\n14\t                                                  resynthesis. The resulting spoofing artifacts can be further shaped by variability in source speech,\n15\t                                                  recording environments, and transmission channels. This variability makes robust generalization\n16\t                                                  across heterogeneous conditions a central requirement for practical detection systems. This report\n17\tarXiv:2607.28351v1 [cs.SD] 30 Jul 2026\n18\t\n19\t\n20\t\n21\t\n22\t                                                  presents Teffic-Audio, a general speech deepfake detection system designed for comprehensive\n23\t                                                  evaluation environment. Teffic-Audio adopts a straightforward detector architecture consisting of\n24\t                                                  a Conformer-based speech encoder, multi-head attentive statistics pooling, and a binary classifier.\n25\t                                                  Rather than relying on additional architectural complexity, the system improves generalization\n26\t                                                  through its training recipe, which integrates multi-source data, attack- and source-balanced sampling,\n27\t                                                  and diverse audio augmentation. Trained only with open-source data, Teffic-Audio achieves a pooled\n28\t                                                  EER of 1.454% on the 14 test sets of Speech-DF-Arena, outperforming all currently public systems\n29\t                                                  on the leaderboard. It also obtains the lowest EER on five individual test sets and shows a favorable\n30\t                                                  performance–complexity trade-off compared with larger leading systems. Overall, Teffic-Audio\n31\t                                                  provides a strong and practical reference system for general speech deepfake detection.\n32\t\n33\t\n34\t                                         Keywords Speech Deepfake Detection · Speech-DF-Arena · Generalization\n35\t\n36\t\n37\t                                         1   Introduction\n38\t\n39\t                                         Recent advances in speech generation Ju et al. [2024], Du et al. [2024a], Chen et al. [2024, 2025] continue to broaden\n40\t                                         the scope of speech deepfake detection. The sources of spoofed speech are no longer confined to a small set of speech\n41\t                                         synthesis or voice conversion methods Wu et al. [2015], Wang et al. [2020]. They now cover generation systems that\n42\t                                         differ in development stage, technical family, and processing pipeline Müller et al. [2024a], Li et al. [2025a]. Recent\n43\t                                         studies also treat vocoder-reconstructed audio Sun et al. [2023], Frank and Schönherr [2021] and audio resynthesized\n44\t                                         after neural codec compression and decoding Xie et al. [2025] as targets in spoofing detection or spoofing-related\n45\t                                         evaluation, which further expands the boundary of the detection problem. The artifacts produced by different generation\n46\t                                         mechanisms are often not consistent Müller et al. [2024b]. Even when spoofing traces appear similar, their observable\n47\t                                         forms can vary with the source of bonafide speech, recording conditions, channel transmission, and compression\n48\t                                         codecs Chandra et al. [2025], Shi et al. [2025]. The central goal of speech deepfake detection therefore shifts from\n49\t                                         identifying specific spoofing methods to maintaining stable and general discriminative ability across diverse bonafide\n50\t                                         speech sources, spoofing mechanisms, and propagation conditions Müller et al. [2024b], Dowerah et al. [2026].\n51\t                                         This change in task scope is also reflected in the evolution of evaluation benchmarks, which move from specific\n52\t                                         anti-spoofing scenarios toward comprehensive generalization assessment. The early ASVspoof series Wu et al. [2015],\n53\t                                         Wang et al. [2020], Yamagishi et al. [2021] mainly formulates evaluation tasks around typical attack scenarios such\n54\t                                         as logical access and physical access. As speech spoofing techniques and application environments continue to\n55\t                                         change, the ADD challenges Yi et al. [2022, 2023] and independent evaluation sets such as In-the-Wild Müller et al.\n56\t                                         [2022] extend the evaluation coverage to more complex audio conditions, including low-quality speech and real-world\n57\t                                         noise. ASVspoof 5 Wang et al. [2024] further organizes evaluation over larger-scale speech sources, attack types, and\n58\t                                         adversarial conditions, imposing stronger requirements on the generalization and robustness of detectors. Nevertheless,\n59\t                                         these evaluations remain largely tied to their own data construction strategies and protocol designs. In contrast to\n60\t\f                                          Teffic-Audio: Tell Fact from Fiction                        T ECHNICAL R EPORT\n61\t\n62\t\n63\t\n64\tbenchmarks centered on a single evaluation source, Speech-DF-Arena Dowerah et al. [2026]1 integrates multiple\n65\trepresentative test sets under a unified protocol and metric system. This design makes cross-dataset stability directly\n66\tcomparable and provides a more suitable comprehensive environment for evaluating general speech deepfake detectors.\n67\tExisting studies improve speech deepfake detection systems along several axes to enhance robustness and general-\n68\tization. Earlier methods mainly strengthen the ability of detectors to capture spoofing cues through spectral feature\n69\tmodeling Wu et al. [2020], Li et al. [2021], end-to-end waveform modeling Tak et al. [2021a], or spectro-temporal\n70\trelation modeling Jung et al. [2022]. With the development of self-supervised speech models, pretrained encoders such\n71\tas wav2vec 2.0 Baevski et al. [2020], WavLM Chen et al. [2022], and XLS-R Babu et al. [2021] have become important\n72\tbases for strong detectors because they provide richer acoustic and speech representations for downstream detection.\n73\tBuilding on these encoders, recent methods further exploit discriminative information across different representation\n74\tlevels, temporal scales, and acoustic conditions through multi-layer feature fusion Zhang et al. [2024], Wang et al.\n75\t[2025], attentive aggregation Truong et al. [2024], Li et al. [2025b], sequence modeling Tran et al. [2025], or expert\n76\trouting Negroni et al. [2025], Pan et al. [2025].\n77\tBeyond model and representation design, data factors also receive increasing attention. Augmentation methods Cohen\n78\tet al. [2022] such as RawBoost Tak et al. [2022a] aim to improve robustness to changes in propagation conditions by\n79\tsimulating channel, codec, and nonlinear distortions. Ge et al. Ge et al. [2025] emphasize the importance of large-scale\n80\tdatasets for adapting pretrained SSL representations. Data-centric methods such as DOSS Huang et al. [2025a] further\n81\tdiscuss the influence of generator diversity and heterogeneous data mixing on cross-dataset generalization. These\n82\tstudies demonstrate that detection performance is shaped by multiple factors. Nevertheless, existing methods are either\n83\tmainly validated on specific benchmarks or limited cross-domain settings Yang et al. [2025], Huang et al. [2025b,\n84\t2026], or have not yet shown sufficiently strong overall performance in a comprehensive multi-dataset evaluation\n85\tenvironment Kulkarni et al. [2026], Laakkonen et al. [2026]. As a result, there is still limited evidence on how to build a\n86\tgeneral speech deepfake detector that can adapt to heterogeneous evaluation scenarios.\n87\tUnder this background, this report introduces Teffic-Audio2 , a general speech deepfake detection system designed\n88\tfor the comprehensive evaluation environment. The system adopts a standard detector architecture, consisting of a\n89\tConformer-based Gulati et al. [2020] speech encoder, multi-head attentive statistics pooling Okabe et al. [2018], and a\n90\tbinary classifier. On this basis, the system focuses on the design of the training procedure, including multi-source data\n91\tconstruction, attack- and source-balanced sampling, and diverse audio augmentation. These designs jointly contribute\n92\tto constructing a more effective training distribution. Our system is trained only with open-source data and achieves\n93\ta pooled EER of 1.454% on the 14 test sets of Speech-DF-Arena; when compared with all currently public systems,\n94\tthis result ranks first. Meanwhile, Teffic-Audio achieves the current lowest EER on 5 individual test sets and shows a\n95\tfavorable performance–complexity trade-off when compared with leading systems on the current public leaderboard.\n96\tThese results provide direct evidence for building a general speech deepfake detector: a simple detector architecture can\n97\tachieve strong generalization when supported by a well-designed training distribution, without necessarily relying on\n98\tincreasingly complex detection architectures. Further ablation studies show that diverse audio augmentation is a key\n99\tfactor in improving the system’s performance in complex and difficult scenarios. In addition, the choices of encoder\n100\tbackbone and pooling layer both have a significant impact on the final system performance. Notably, we also find that a\n101\tsystem variant with only 4 Conformer blocks can still achieve a pooled EER of 3.346%, indicating that the system can\n102\tmaintain strong cross-dataset generalization with substantially reduced encoder depth.\n103\t\n104\t\n105\t2       System Overview\n106\t\n107\tThe system targets utterance-level speech deepfake detection. Given an input speech utterance, the model outputs a\n108\tdetection score that represents the likelihood of the utterance being spoofed speech. The overall architecture consists of\n109\ta SSL-initialized speech encoder, a pooling layer, and a binary classifier, forming an end-to-end discriminative model.\n110\tThe system architecture is illustrated on the right side of Figure 1.\n111\tThe speech encoder is initialized from w2v-BERT 2.0 Barrault et al. [2023]. Its backbone comprises a CNN-based\n112\tfeature extractor followed by 24 Conformer blocks Gulati et al. [2020], each with a model dimension of 1024. The\n113\tencoder converts the input waveform into contextualized frame-level speech representations.\n114\tOn top of the frame-level representations, the system adopts multi-head attentive statistics pooling (MHASP) Okabe\n115\tet al. [2018] to obtain a fixed-dimensional utterance-level representation. MHASP uses 4 attention heads to compute\n116\tattention weights along the temporal dimension and estimates the weighted mean and weighted standard deviation\n117\taccordingly. The statistics from all heads are concatenated to form a 2048-dimensional pooling representation, which is\n118\t    1\n119\t        https://huggingface.co/spaces/Speech-Arena-2025/Speech-DF-Arena\n120\t    2\n121\t        Demo page: https://tefficlabs.com/teffic-audio\n122\t\n123\t\n124\t                                                            2\n125\t\f                                                           Teffic-Audio: Tell Fact from Fiction                                                   T ECHNICAL R EPORT\n126\t\n127\t\n128\t\n129\t                                    TRAINING RECIPE                                                                   SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE\n130\t\n131\t       Speech Deepfake Corpus\n132\t                                                                                                                                       Multi-head Attentive\n133\t                                                            Diverse Audio Augmentation                                                  Statistics Pooling\n134\t\n135\t                                      Bonafide   Spoofed     RawBoost          RIR\n136\t                                       (50%)      (50%)                                                                Frame-level     Head 1\n137\t                                                                                                                     Representations\n138\t                                                              MUSAN           Filter                                                                               MLP\n139\t                                                                                                       …                               Head 2\n140\t                                                                                                                              …\n141\t                ……                                                                                                                                               Classifier\n142\t                                                             Pitch Shift   Time Masking\n143\t                                                                                                                                       Head 3\n144\t                                                                                           Conformer-based Encoder\n145\t                                                              Codec                                                                       …                       Sigmoid\n146\t    Supplementary Bonafide Corpus      ……         ……        Compression     Packet Loss\n147\t\n148\t                                     Balance Mini-batch                                                                                Head N                 Spoofing Score\n149\t                                                                 ……\n150\t\n151\t\n152\t\n153\t                ……\n154\t\n155\t\n156\t         Figure 1: Overview of the Teffic-Audio system architecture and the waveform-to-score training pipeline.\n157\t\n158\t\n159\tthen projected by an MLP with hidden dimensions of 2048 → 1536 → 1024. This design aggregates discriminative\n160\tinformation along the temporal dimension from multiple attention views.\n161\tFinally, the system uses an MLP classifier with a hidden dimension of 512 to map the 1024-dimensional global\n162\trepresentation to binary classification logits. After sigmoid activation, the posterior probability of spoofed speech is\n163\tused as the detection score. During training, the model is optimized with the binary cross-entropy (BCE) loss:\n164\t                                                                      N\n165\t                                                              1 X\n166\t                                                 LBCE = −           [yi log pi + (1 − yi ) log(1 − pi )] ,                                                                  (1)\n167\t                                                              N i=1\n168\t\n169\twhere yi ∈ 0, 1 denotes the binary label of the i-th sample, and pi denotes the posterior probability that the sample\n170\tis predicted as spoofed speech. This objective is applied directly to the complete detection path, enabling the speech\n171\tencoder, pooling layer, and classifier to be jointly optimized in an end-to-end manner. During inference, this probability\n172\tis directly used as the final score without additional post-processing.\n173\t\n174\t\n175\t3       Training Recipe\n176\t\n177\tThe training recipe is a central component of the system design. In a comprehensive evaluation environment, the\n178\tmain challenge arises from distributional heterogeneity caused jointly by different spoofing mechanisms, bonafide\n179\tspeech conditions, and propagation chains. This section describes how the training procedure is designed to obtain\n180\ta more comprehensive supervision distribution. As illustrated on the left side of Figure 1, the system is developed\n181\tfrom three aspects: Multi-source Training Corpus extends the source coverage of bonafide and spoofed speech, Attack-\n182\tand Source-Balanced Data Sampling adjusts the gradient contributions of different spoofing mechanisms and bonafide\n183\tspeech sources, and Diverse Audio Augmentation increases the observable variation of training samples under different\n184\tacoustic environments and transmission conditions.\n185\t\n186\t3.1      Multi-source Training Corpus\n187\t\n188\tA single dataset usually reflects only limited speech sources, collection conditions, and spoofing methods, making it\n189\tinsufficient to support cross-domain generalization in comprehensive evaluation. To reduce source bias introduced\n190\tby the training corpus itself, the system integrates multi-source open-source speech data. Overall, the training corpus\n191\tconsists of two parts. The first part is the speech deepfake corpus, which contains public speech deepfake detection\n192\tdatasets and covers major spoofing mechanisms, including text-to-speech (TTS), voice conversion (VC), neural vocoder\n193\treconstruction (NV), and neural codec resynthesis (NC). The second part is the supplementary bonafide corpus, which\n194\tconsists of additional real speech datasets and is used to expand the coverage of bonafide speech in terms of source,\n195\tspeaker, language, and recording condition. Table 1 summarizes the main sources, languages, sample sizes, and\n196\tgenerator metadata of the training corpus.\n197\t\n198\t\n199\t                                                                                       3\n200\t\f                                         Teffic-Audio: Tell Fact from Fiction                         T ECHNICAL R EPORT\n201\t\n202\t\n203\t\n204\tTable 1: Multi-source training corpus used in the proposed system. # Bonafide and # Spoofed denote the numbers of\n205\treal and spoofed training utterances. Generator Types denotes coarse spoofing mechanisms, text-to-speech (TTS),\n206\tvoice conversion (VC), neural vocoder reconstruction (NV), and neural codec resynthesis (NC). # Generator is the\n207\tnumber of distinct spoof generators in metadata when annotated.\n208\t\n209\tDataset                                    Year     Language     # Bonafide   # Spoofed Generator Types # Generator\n210\t                                               Speech Deepfake Corpus\n211\tASVspoof2015 [Wu et al., 2015]             2015       EN            9,404        184,000      TTS, VC             10\n212\tASVspoof2019LA [Wang et al., 2020]         2019       EN            2,580         22,800      TTS, VC              6\n213\tASVspoof5 [Wang et al., 2024]              2024       EN           18,797        163,560      TTS, VC              8\n214\tADD2022 [Yi et al., 2022]                  2022       ZH            3,312         24,772      TTS, VC              –\n215\tADD2023 Track1 [Yi et al., 2023]           2023       ZH                –         24,072      TTS, VC              –\n216\tFakeOrReal [Reimao and Tzerpos, 2019]      2019       EN           26,900         26,900        TTS                –\n217\tSpoofCeleb [Jung et al., 2025]             2024       EN         230,948       2,309,473        TTS               10\n218\tReplayDF [Müller et al., 2025]             2025     MULTI          26,160         26,160        TTS                4\n219\tDFADD [Du et al., 2024b]                   2024       EN           44,455        163,500        TTS                5\n220\tMLAAD [Müller et al., 2024a]               2024     MULTI               –        172,393        TTS               58\n221\tLibriSeVoc [Sun et al., 2023]              2023       EN           13,201         79,206        NV                6\n222\tSpeechFake [Huang et al., 2025c]           2025     EN, ZH         75,708        629,154    TTS, VC, NV           30\n223\tWavefake [Frank and Schönherr, 2021]       2021     EN, JA          9,170        117,985        NV                6\n224\tCodecFake [Xie et al., 2025]               2024     EN, ZH       105,821         634,926        NC                 7\n225\t                                            Supplementary Bonafide Corpus\n226\tLibriSpeech [Panayotov et al., 2015]       2015       EN          281,241              –          –                –\n227\tAISHELL3 [Shi et al., 2020]                2020       ZH           88,035              –          –                –\n228\tGigaSpeech [Chen et al., 2021]             2021       EN          885,397              –          –                –\n229\tCNCeleb [Fan et al., 2020]                 2019       ZH          524,787              –          –                –\n230\tCommonVoice [Ardila et al., 2020]          2020     MULTI         167,571              –          –                –\n231\t\n232\t\n233\t       • Speech Deepfake Corpus. The speech deepfake corpus brings together speech spoofing data from different\n234\t         development stages. Early data mainly come from ASVspoof2015 and ASVspoof2019LA, which are used to\n235\t         cover typical TTS and VC attacks in the logical access scenario. The training corpus then incorporates datasets\n236\t         such as ADD2022, ADD2023 Track1, FakeOrReal, SpoofCeleb, ReplayDF, and ASVspoof5 to supplement\n237\t         samples from more complex challenge protocols and noisy conditions. Furthermore, the system introduces\n238\t         DFADD, MLAAD , LibriSeVoc, SpeechFake, Wavefake, and CodecFake to provide spoofing-related samples\n239\t         from more recent or more specific generation pipelines. These data mainly involve diffusion / flow-matching\n240\t         TTS, multilingual TTS, neural vocoder generation or reconstruction, and neural codec reconstruction, thereby\n241\t         extending the coverage of the training corpus to new generation and reconstruction pipelines.\n242\t       • Supplementary Bonafide Corpus. In addition to the bonafide speech included in the speech deepfake corpus,\n243\t         this system further introduces a supplementary bonafide corpus to enhance the distributional coverage of real\n244\t         speech. Recent studies Kwok et al. [2025] show that models trained on existing deepfake detection data can\n245\t         suffer performance degradation under complex real-speech conditions, and some bonafide speech may be\n246\t         misclassified as spoofed speech due to differences in noise, recording environment, or speaking style. Based\n247\t         on this observation, we explicitly increase the source diversity of bonafide speech during training to reduce the\n248\t         dependence of the model on limited real-speech conditions.\n249\t         Specifically, the supplementary corpus is divided into two categories. The first category contains relatively\n250\t         standard read-speech recordings, including LibriSpeech and AISHELL3, which are used to supplement clean\n251\t         and large-scale bonafide speech. The second category contains real speech data from more open sources and\n252\t         more complex scenarios, including GigaSpeech, CommonVoice, and CNCeleb, which are used to introduce\n253\t         stronger variations in speaker, language, content, and recording condition.\n254\t\n255\t3.2   Attack- and Source-Balanced Data Sampling\n256\t\n257\tAs shown in Table 1, different training data sources contribute substantially different numbers of utterances, and the\n258\tnumber of attack generators covered on the spoofed speech side also varies. If all samples are directly merged and\n259\trandomly sampled, the training process can be dominated by a small number of large-scale data sources or frequent\n260\tattack generators. This may cause the model to overfit specific source distributions and produce clear performance\n261\t\n262\t\n263\t                                                            4\n264\t\f                                           Teffic-Audio: Tell Fact from Fiction                          T ECHNICAL R EPORT\n265\t\n266\t\n267\t\n268\tTable 2: Audio augmentation operators used during training. For each sample, the system applies augmentation with\n269\tprobability 0.5 by randomly selecting one or two operators.\n270\t\n271\tOperator                Setting                                          Simulated condition\n272\t                                       Acoustic- and Recording-related Augmentation\n273\tRawBoost                                                                 Device/channel nonlinearity, impulsive disturbance,\n274\t                        Algorithms 1, 2, and 3\n275\t[Tak et al., 2022a]                                                      and colored background noise.\n276\tRIR                     Room impulse responses                           Reverberant recordings in enclosed spaces.\n277\t[Ko et al., 2017]\n278\tMUSAN                   Noise: 5–30 dB; Speech: 15–30 dB                 Additive background interference from environmental\n279\t[Snyder et al., 2015]   Music: 10–30 dB                                  noise, competing speech, or music.\n280\tPitch Shift             Pitch offset: [−1, +1] semitones                 Small global pitch offsets across processing chains.\n281\t                                    Transmission- and Platform-related Augmentation\n282\tFiltering               Low-pass: 2.2–7.2 kHz; High-pass: 70–360 Hz    Narrowband or frequency-shaped transmission paths.\n283\t                        Band-pass: 300–4000 Hz\n284\tTime Masking            One waveform segment with 1–10% duration         Brief local dropouts or corrupted waveform segments.\n285\t                        MP3, Vorbis, Opus, AAC, Speex, GSM, G.711        Lossy re-encoding by streaming, messaging, or upload\n286\tCodec Compression\n287\t                        G.723.1, and Encodec [Défossez et al., 2022]     pipelines.\n288\t                                                                         VoIP packet loss with silence or repetition\n289\tPacket Loss             Loss rate: 0.01–0.1; Packet length: 20 ms\n290\t                                                                         concealment.\n291\t\n292\t\n293\t\n294\ttrade-offs across different evaluation sets. Similar data mixing issues have also been widely discussed in multi-domain\n295\tpretraining and multilingual learning Xie et al. [2023], Chung et al., Ye et al. [2025], where the sampling strategy\n296\tdirectly affects the generalization ability and robustness of the model.\n297\tTo reduce such data distribution bias, this system adopts attack- and source-balanced data sampling to construct the\n298\ttraining distribution of each epoch. For spoofed speech, the system prioritizes the attack generator labels provided by\n299\tthe datasets as sampling units. If such labels are not available, the dataset itself is used as the sampling unit. Let M\n300\tdenote the number of samples for each spoofing unit. In this system, we set M = 1000. If there are K spoofing units\n301\tin total, the number of spoofed samples in the epoch is M K. The system then samples the same number of bonafide\n302\tspeech samples and distributes them uniformly across different real speech datasets. This strategy jointly balances\n303\tbonafide and spoofed speech, attack generators on the spoofed side, and data sources on the bonafide side.\n304\t\n305\t3.3   Diverse Audio Augmentation\n306\t\n307\tIn real applications, speech signals often undergo various processing procedures caused by recording devices, room\n308\tacoustics, background noise, bandwidth limitations, platform compression, and network transmission. These factors\n309\tmay change the observable form of spoofing artifacts and may also make bonafide speech exhibit acoustic conditions\n310\tdifferent from those in the training set. To improve the robustness of the detector to changes in propagation chains,\n311\tthis system introduces diverse audio augmentation at the waveform level, thereby expanding the coverage of signal\n312\tconditions during training.\n313\t\n314\t       • Acoustic- and Recording-related Augmentation. The system first considers variations introduced by\n315\t         acoustic environments and recording devices. RawBoost is used to simulate nonlinear channel responses,\n316\t         impulsive perturbations, and colored background noise. Pitch shift introduces slight global pitch changes, RIR\n317\t         convolution introduces room reverberation, and MUSAN mixes noise, speech, or music into the input speech\n318\t         with a random signal-to-noise ratio. These augmentations jointly cover source signals, device responses,\n319\t         spatial acoustics, and external interference, preventing the model from relying only on spoofing cues observed\n320\t         under clean close-talking conditions.\n321\t       • Transmission- and Platform-related Augmentation. The system further considers changes that may occur\n322\t         during speech transmission, uploading, forwarding, and platform processing. Filtering covers low-pass,\n323\t         high-pass, and band-pass conditions, corresponding to transmission paths with bandwidth limitations or\n324\t         frequency-response changes. Time masking simulates local waveform missingness or corruption. Codec\n325\t         compression covers multiple lossy speech or audio coding conditions, while packet loss simulates short packet\n326\t\n327\t\n328\t                                                               5\n329\t\f                                          Teffic-Audio: Tell Fact from Fiction                        T ECHNICAL R EPORT\n330\t\n331\t\n332\t\n333\t         drops and concealment strategies in VoIP transmission. This class of augmentations is used to improve the\n334\t         robustness of the model to propagation-chain changes and platform-processing distortions.\n335\t\n336\t\n337\t4     Benchmark Evaluation\n338\t\n339\t4.1   Evaluation Protocol\n340\t\n341\tThis report uses Speech-DF-Arena Dowerah et al. [2026] as the main evaluation benchmark. The platform aggregates\n342\tmultiple public evaluation sets under a unified evaluation protocol, and we follow its reported results on 14 test sets\n343\tto evaluate the general detection ability of the model. The evaluation sets cover the ASVspoof series, including\n344\tASVspoof19 Wang et al. [2020], ASVspoof21-LA, ASVspoof21-DF Yamagishi et al. [2021], and ASVspoof24-\n345\tEval Wang et al. [2024]; the ADD challenge series, including ADD 2022 Track 1 (ADD22-T1), ADD 2022 Track 3\n346\t(ADD22-T3) Yi et al. [2022], ADD 2023 Round 1 (ADD23-R1), and ADD 2023 Round 2 (ADD23-R2) Yi et al. [2023];\n347\tas well as In-the-Wild (ITW) Müller et al. [2022], FakeOrReal (FoR) Reimao and Tzerpos [2019], CodecFake (CF) Xie\n348\tet al. [2025], LibriSeVoc (LSV) Sun et al. [2023], DFADD Du et al. [2024b], and SONAR Li et al. [2024]. These test\n349\tsets span diverse corpus compositions, spoofing generation pipelines, and signal processing conditions.\n350\tIt should be noted that the training corpus used in this report includes the official training partitions of some benchmarks\n351\tin the evaluation suite. However, many of these benchmarks have clear train–test distribution gaps in spoofing methods,\n352\tgeneration models, and other signal-processing conditions. Therefore, the evaluation does not reduce to a purely\n353\tin-distribution test and still provides evidence of the model’s ability to generalize to unseen attacks.\n354\tSpeech-DF-Arena uses EER, ACC, and F1-score as the main evaluation metrics, and reports dataset-level, pooled, and\n355\taverage results separately. Dataset-level results are computed independently on each test set. Pooled results merge\n356\tall scores, determine a unified threshold, and compute the metrics accordingly; these results are used as the basis for\n357\tleaderboard ranking. Average results are obtained by directly averaging the dataset-level results across all test sets.\n358\t\n359\t4.2   Leaderboard Performance\n360\t\n361\tTable 3 compares the EER performance of Teffic-Audio with the public systems in the current public snapshot of\n362\tthe official Speech-DF-Arena leaderboard. Complete ACC and F1-score results are provided in Appendix Tables 6\n363\tand 7. In terms of pooled EER, Teffic-Audio achieves 1.454%, which is lower than all currently public systems.\n364\tCompared with the top three systems, Modulate-VELMA-2-Synthetic-Voice Modulate [2026] (1.586%), Resemble-\n365\tDetect-3B-Omni Resemble AI [2026a] (2.099%), and Hiya-Authenticity-Verification-Multi-v1 Hiya [2026] (2.324%),\n366\tTeffic-Audio reduces the pooled EER by approximately 8.3%, 30.7%, and 37.4% in relative terms, respectively. This\n367\tresult indicates that, under a mainstream detector architecture, the proposed training recipe can provide the detection\n368\tsystem with strong cross-domain discriminative ability.\n369\tFrom the perspective of individual test sets, Teffic-Audio achieves the lowest EER on five test sets, including CodecFake,\n370\tADD 2022 Track 3, ADD 2023 R1, DFADD , and LibriSeVoc, and maintains low error rates on challenging test sets\n371\tsuch as ADD 2022 Track 1 and ASV2024-Eval. This shows that the performance of the system is not supported only by\n372\tadvantages on a few test sets, but instead remains stable across multiple types of evaluation conditions. When model size\n373\tis further considered, Teffic-Audio has 590.0M parameters, which is only larger than Modulate-VELMA-2-Synthetic-\n374\tVoice and is substantially smaller than Resemble-Detect-3B-Omni (3B) and Hiya-Authenticity-Verification-Multi-v1\n375\t(1B). This suggests that the proposed system not only achieves strong spoofing detection performance, but also presents\n376\ta favorable performance–complexity trade-off.\n377\t\n378\t\n379\t5     Ablation Analysis\n380\t\n381\t5.1   Effect of the Training Recipe\n382\t\n383\tTable 4 presents the impact of different training strategies on system performance under the fixed architecture. The\n384\tbaseline simply merges multiple speech deepfake datasets and applies random sampling during training, yielding a\n385\tpooled EER of 7.159% and an average EER of 3.693%. The clear gap between this baseline and the final system indicates\n386\tthat merely increasing the diversity of training data sources is insufficient for robust cross-dataset generalization. After\n387\tintroducing attack- and source-balanced sampling, the pooled EER decreases to 6.456%. This suggests that explicitly\n388\tbalancing attack types and data sources can alleviate distributional bias in multi-source training. With the further\n389\tinclusion of the supplementary bonafide corpus, the pooled EER is reduced to 5.435%. This result demonstrates that a\n390\tgeneral-purpose detector requires diverse spoofed samples as well as broad coverage of bonafide speech distributions.\n391\t\n392\t\n393\t                                                             6\n394\t\f                                                                  Teffic-Audio: Tell Fact from Fiction                                                      T ECHNICAL R EPORT\n395\t\n396\t\n397\t\n398\tTable 3: EER(%) performance on Speech-DF-Arena. The table reports Teffic-Audio and all systems from the current\n399\tpublic leaderboard snapshot, covering licence, parameter size, overall and dataset-level EER results on the 14 evaluation\n400\tsets. Params denotes the number of parameters in millions. Pooled and Avg. denote pooled EER and the average of\n401\tdataset-level EERs, respectively. Systems are ranked by pooled EER.\n402\t                                                                                           ASVspoof                                        ADD\n403\tSystem                            Licence      Params Pooled     Avg.    ITW                                     FoR      CF                             DFADD            LSV      SONAR\n404\t                                                                                  19     21-LA 21-DF     24-E                    22-T1 22-T3 23-R1 23-R2\n405\t\n406\tTeffic-Audio                     Proprietary   590.0    1.454    1.236   1.321   0.242   2.038   0.262  