Kulkarni et al., 2024]\n469\tRawnet2 [Tak et al., 2021a]        Open         17.6    45.996 47.567 49.187 33.039 40.071 40.669 41.219 65.680 50.219 49.359 47.627 55.641 64.554               39.070   48.167   43.009\n470\t\n471\t\n472\t\n473\t\n474\tFor data augmentation, we compare two schemes: using RawBoost alone and using the Diverse Audio Augmentation\n475\tstrategy adopted in this work. Compared with the system after incorporating the supplementary bonafide corpus, using\n476\tRawBoost alone does not yield consistent or substantial improvements in the overall metrics. A closer examination\n477\tof individual test sets shows that RawBoost brings clear gains mainly on scenarios such as ASVspoof 2021-LA and\n478\tASVspoof 2021-DF. However, it provides limited improvement or even causes degradation on datasets like ASVspoof\n479\t2024-Eval. This suggests that RawBoost can simulate certain channel and noise perturbations as a commonly used\n480\twaveform-level augmentation method in speech deepfake detection. Nevertheless, it is insufficient to consistently\n481\timprove the cross-dataset generalization of a general-purpose detector under a comprehensive evaluation setting.\n482\tIn contrast, Diverse Audio Augmentation reduces the pooled EER to 1.454% and the average EER to 1.236%, making it\n483\ta key factor in improving system generalization. The performance gains are particularly pronounced on test sets that are\n484\tmore sensitive to coding compression and channel variations. Specifically, the EER on ASVspoof 2024-Eval decreases\n485\tfrom 17.814% to 1.405%, while that on ADD 2022-Track1 decreases from 13.794% to 7.031%. These results indicate\n486\tthat richer audio augmentation can substantially improve the model’s adaptability to complex acoustic conditions. At\n487\t\n488\t\n489\t                                                                                             7\n490\t\f                                                         Teffic-Audio: Tell Fact from Fiction                                                    T ECHNICAL R EPORT\n491\t\n492\t\n493\t\n494\tTable 4: Ablation on the training recipe. Architecture is fixed to the w2v-BERT 2.0 encoder with MHASP throughout.\n495\tBaseline uses merged speech deepfake corpus with random sampling only.\n496\t                                                                                   ASVspoof                                    ADD\n497\tConfiguration                           Params Pooled Avg.      ITW                                       FoR   CF                           DFADD LSV SONAR\n498\t                                                                         19     21-LA 21-DF        24-E              22-T1 22-T3 23-R1 23-R2\n499\tBaseline                                590.0   7.159   3.693 1.445 0.600 5.825             1.619 19.650 0.345 0.097 14.885 1.915     1.185   3.655   0.017   0.000   0.459\n500\t + Attack- & Source-balanced Sampling   590.0   6.456   3.539   1.173   0.243   6.822       0.540 17.840 0.216 0.144 14.730   1.352   1.502   4.603   0.000   0.000   0.377\n501\t  + Supplementary Bonafide Corpus       590.0   5.435   3.289   1.242   0.598   7.972       1.304 13.630 0.193 0.235 14.038   1.557   0.581   3.509   0.000   0.000   1.183\n502\t    + RawBoost Augmentation             590.0   5.896   3.081   1.187   0.178   4.552       0.347 17.814 0.410 0.268 13.794   1.093   0.439   2.046   0.000   0.000   1.006\n503\t    + Diverse Audio Augmentation        590.0   1.454   1.236   1.321   0.242   2.038       0.262 1.405 0.972 0.748 7.031     0.837   0.356   1.836   0.000   0.000   0.251\n504\t\n505\t\n506\t\n507\t\n508\tthe same time, no systematic degradation is observed on other test sets. This suggests that the proposed strategy does\n509\tnot weaken the model’s ability to discriminate spoofing cues under conventional speech conditions, thereby leading to\n510\tstronger cross-dataset robustness.\n511\t\n512\t5.2    Effect of the Model Architecture\n513\t\n514\tTable 5 presents the ablation study on model architecture under our training recipe. We examine three aspects of the\n515\tdetector design: the choice of speech encoder, the utterance-level pooling layer, and the encoder depth of Teffic-Audio.\n516\t\n517\t           • Encoder Backbone. We first compare the impact of different encoder backbones on system performance.\n518\t             Although traditional convolutional models such as AASIST Jung et al. [2022] and Res2Net Li et al. [2021]\n519\t             have relatively small parameter sizes, they perform substantially worse than detectors based on large-scale\n520\t             SSL-initialized encoders in the comprehensive evaluation setting. Introducing such encoders leads to clear\n521\t             performance improvements. For example, WavLM Large Chen et al. [2022] + MHASP and XLS-R 1B Xiao\n522\t             and Das [2025] + MHASP achieve pooled EERs of 4.332% and 4.419%, respectively, outperforming AASIST\n523\t             and Res2Net.\n524\t             A further comparison between different scales of the same encoder family shows that WavLM Base + MHASP\n525\t             and XLS-R 300M + MHASP obtain pooled EERs of 9.446% and 6.079%, respectively, both significantly\n526\t             higher than their corresponding large-scale variants. This indicates that increasing the encoder scale generally\n527\t             helps improve system performance. Among different SSL-initialized backbones, the w2v-BERT 2.0 encoder\n528\t             adopted in Teffic-Audio achieves the best result, further reducing the pooled EER to 1.454%. It also shows\n529\t             stronger generalization across multiple test sets. These results suggest that the performance of general-purpose\n530\t             speech deepfake detection is not determined solely by parameter size. It is also closely related to the encoder’s\n531\t             pretraining objective and acoustic representation capability.\n532\t             From another perspective, under the same evaluation protocol, models trained with our recipe also substantially\n533\t             outperform leaderboard systems that use similar backbones. Specifically, AASIST achieves a pooled EER of\n534\t             15.668%, corresponding to a 52.2% reduction compared with the leaderboard AASIST system. WavLM Base +\n535\t             MHASP achieves 9.446%, reducing the EER by 71.7% compared with WavLM-ECAPA [Kulkarni et al., 2024].\n536\t             XLS-R 300M + MHASP achieves 6.079%, reducing the EER by 62.2% compared with XLSR+SLS [Zhang\n537\t             et al., 2024]. These results further indicate that the proposed training recipe can effectively improve the overall\n538\t             performance of different encoder backbones in general-purpose deepfake detection.\n539\t           • Pooling Layer. We next compare the impact of different utterance-level pooling layers. With the w2v-BERT\n540\t             2.0 encoder fixed, mean pooling and ASP achieve pooled EERs of 2.269% and 2.339%, respectively. Both\n541\t             yield higher EERs than the final MHASP-based system, indicating that the choice of pooling layer also\n542\t             affects overall system performance. Notably, although ASP introduces attention-based pooling, it does not\n543\t             improve over mean pooling. This suggests that simple frame-level weighting may be insufficient to exploit the\n544\t             fine-grained representations produced by the encoder. In contrast, MHASP aggregates frame-level statistics\n545\t             from different subspaces through multiple attention heads, which provides a more effective utterance-level\n546\t             representation for cross-dataset generalization.\n547\t           • Encoder Depth. The preceding backbone comparison shows that directly adopting smaller SSL backbones\n548\t             leads to clear performance degradation. We therefore further examine whether the Teffic-Audio encoder\n549\t             can be made shallower to reduce parameter size while maintaining stable cross-dataset generalization. To\n550\t             this end, we train and evaluate variants with different numbers of Conformer blocks. The results show that\n551\t             overall performance improves as encoder depth increases. From N = 3 to the full 24-layer configuration, the\n552\t             pooled EER gradually decreases from 4.735% to 1.454%. This result indicates that, after selecting an effective\n553\t             backbone, encoder depth remains a key factor in the trade-off between performance and complexity.\n554\t             Further examination on individual test sets shows that the benefits of increasing depth are not uniform across\n555\t             all evaluation scenarios. On test sets such as ITW, ASVspoof21-DF, DFADD, and LSV, the 4-layer or 6-layer\n556\t\n557\t\n558\t                                                                                        8\n559\t\f                                                      Teffic-Audio: Tell Fact from Fiction                                                 T ECHNICAL R EPORT\n560\t\n561\t\n562\t\n563\tTable 5: Ablation study on model architecture. Teffic-Audio refers to the full system using MHASP and a 24-layer\n564\tencoder initialized from w2v-BERT 2.0. All metric columns report EER (%). MP and ASP denote mean pooling and\n565\tattentive statistics pooling, respectively. For Teffic-Audio depth variants, N denotes the encoder depth, and the N -layer\n566\tencoders are initialized from the first N Conformer blocks of w2v-BERT 2.0.\n567\t                                                                      ASVspoof                                          ADD\n568\tConfiguration              Params Pooled    Avg.    ITW                                       FoR      CF                             DFADD LSV SONAR\n569\t                                                             1