 3.280   1.459   2.423   6.449    1.835     9.901 2.785 0.594      13.075   1.340   0.798   3.683   0.000   0.000   1.582\n583\tTeffic-Audio (N =4)         106.4   3.346   2.415   1.083   0.699   4.244    0.593     6.744 4.726 0.595      10.734   1.059   0.440   2.230   0.000   0.000   0.658\n584\tTeffic-Audio (N =6)         154.8   2.839   2.005   0.922   0.515   3.134    0.484     5.306 2.633 0.937       9.640   0.918   0.526   2.153   0.000   0.000   0.910\n585\tTeffic-Audio (N =12)        299.9   2.317   1.965   1.030   0.565   3.307    0.455     3.769 4.057 1.279       8.754   0.826   0.576   2.324   0.000   0.000   0.564\n586\t\n587\t\n588\t\n589\t\n590\t               configurations already achieve relatively low EERs. This suggests that spoofing cues in many milder test\n591\t               conditions can already be captured by a shallower encoder. In contrast, the advantage of the full-depth model\n592\t               is mainly observed on more challenging test sets, such as ASVspoof24-E and ADD22-T1. For example, the\n593\t               EER on ASVspoof24-E decreases from 6.744% with N = 4 to 1.405% with the full model. The EER on\n594\t               ADD22-T1 decreases from 10.734% to 7.031%. These results suggest that increasing encoder depth mainly\n595\t               improves the model’s adaptability to challenging scenarios. Meanwhile, a clear degradation is observed when\n596\t               reducing the depth from N = 4 to N = 3. The pooled EER increases from 3.346% to 4.735%, corresponding\n597\t               to a relative increase of 41.5%. This indicates that overly reducing the encoder depth weakens the stability of\n598\t               cross-dataset discrimination.\n599\t               It is worth noting that shallower Teffic-Audio configurations still retain strong performance. The 4-layer\n600\t               configuration contains only 106.4M parameters, yet achieves a pooled EER of 3.346%. This outperforms\n601\t               WavLM Base Chen et al. [2022] + MHASP, which has a comparable parameter size. The 6-layer configuration\n602\t               further achieves a pooled EER of 2.839% with 154.8M parameters. This performance is close to leading\n603\t               systems on the leaderboard, such as Resemble-Detect-3B-Omni and Hiya-Authenticity-Verification-Multi-\n604\t               v1. These results indicate that reducing the depth of an effective backbone provides a better performance–\n605\t               complexity trade-off than directly replacing it with a smaller SSL encoder. Combined with an appropriate\n606\t               training recipe, shallower configurations can still maintain strong cross-dataset generalization.\n607\t\n608\t\n609\t\n610\t6    Conclusion\n611\t\n612\tThis report presented Teffic-Audio, a general speech deepfake detection system for comprehensive evaluation envi-\n613\tronment. The system follows a standard detection pipeline and focuses on the construction of an effective training\n614\tdistribution. Trained only with open-source data, Teffic-Audio achieves a pooled EER of 1.454% on the 14 test\n615\tsets of Speech-DF-Arena and ranks first compared with all currently public systems. Ablation studies show that the\n616\tfinal performance cannot be attributed merely to large-scale data aggregation. Balanced sampling, supplementary\n617\tbonafide speech, and especially diverse audio augmentation all contribute to stronger cross-dataset generalization.\n618\tThe architecture analysis further shows that the encoder backbone and pooling layer have clear effects on system\n619\tperformance. In particular, reducing the depth of the system backbone still yields competitive results while substantially\n620\tdecreasing the number of parameters. These findings show that Teffic-Audio provides an effective and practical baseline\n621\tfor general speech deepfake detection under comprehensive evaluation environment.\n622\t\n623\t\n624\tAuthors\n625\t\n626\tContributors: Wan 